SanjeevaniDock 🌿
Rare Disease Phytochemical Docking Platform

SanjeevaniDock is an advanced, full-stack computational chemistry web application built with Streamlit. It bridges traditional Ayurvedic pharmacology with modern structure-based drug discovery pipelines, specifically tailored for screening plant-based phytochemicals against rare and orphan disease protein targets.

Developed by: Ms. Amisha Bilakar & Team

🚀 Key Features
The application operates in a robust 4-phase computational pipeline:

Phase 1: Baseline Native Docking

Dynamically loads target proteins from the RCSB PDB and ligand metadata from NIH PubChem based on a built-in rare disease database.

Executes automated molecular docking using a built-in AutoDock Vina engine.

Applies memory-safe Universal Force Field (UFF) minimization via RDKit to resolve steric clashes and relax complexes.

Phase 2: Generative Scaffold Structural Redesign

Analyzes the active phytochemical SMILES matrix and categorizes its molecular scaffold.

Utilizes a computational cleaving engine to safely attach new functional fragments (e.g., methylation, epoxidation), generating an optimized derivative library.

Phase 3: ADMET 3.0 Pharmacokinetics Profiling

Evaluates the physiological properties of redesigned molecules.

Calculates Lipinski's Rule of 5 compliance, TPSA, LogP, permeability, and volume.

Generates a mock FTIR vibrational spectrum for structural topology footprints.

Phase 4: Post-Redesign Validation & Master Synthesis

Docks the newly redesigned derivatives back into the target receptor.

Performs a direct thermodynamic side-by-side comparison (Gibbs free energy and UFF delta) to issue a definitive Master Synthesis Verdict.

Exports publication-ready, interactive HTML Research Reports containing 3D models and contact matrices.
